# chip_stack — the ON-GRID STACKED chip variant, BOTTOM-UP.
#
# Same two cell types as chip.bdb.sql, placed deliberately instead of by SA:
#
#   LEFT  column  x=[0..6100]      2 x big2, pitch 7560  (cell 6300 + ch 1260)
#                                  y = 0, 7560
#   RIGHT column  x=[7100..9430]   4 x mix2, pitch 3528  (cell 2360 + ch 1168)
#                                  y = 458, 3986, 7514, 11042
#   die 9430 x 13860, 100 top-level cross-hierarchy buses, 11750 nets
#
# MIRROR-SYMMETRIC, ON GRID.  Columns are CENTRED (--column-align center: mix2
# gets 458 margin top and bottom, which is forced — zero margins are
# arithmetically impossible when the channels must be ≡160 mod 504) and the
# three instances above y=6930 are FLIPPED upside down (--mirror-upper), so all
# 488 leaf blocks map exactly onto their reflections about the centreline.
#
# THE MIRROR NEEDS A MIRROR-SYMMETRIC TECHNOLOGY, which is why this vehicle
# sources chip_tracks_MIRROR.buda rather than chip_tracks.buda.  A flipped
# instance's routing is its reference's reflected about the instance's own
# centreline, so the H patterns must be invariant under that reflection.  In
# chip_tracks they are invariant under NO reflection at all: every rail has a
# wider gap after it than before it, so the repeating unit is not a palindrome
# and no axis exists at any offset.  (M6 is the exception — gaps 1 on both
# sides — and it is the one H layer there that does have an axis.)
#
# chip_tracks_mirror.buda centres each rail in its own whitespace and shifts
# three H origins so the axes land on the flipped instances' centrelines.
# Periods, widths, signal counts, overheads and the 504 grid are all unchanged.
# Both cells then report ALIGNED and solve-once-copy carries the mirror:
#
#   asymmetric tracks:  3262 bits copied to 100 siblings, 18089 solved alone
#   symmetric tracks:  18122 bits copied to 380 siblings,  3334 solved alone
#
# `on_mismatch independent` is kept as a safety net, not because it is needed.
# Endpoints, the isolating control, and the retraction of some earlier numbers
# are in flow/chip/ReadMe.md.
#
# WHY IT IS ON GRID.  Every instance of a cell shares ONE x, so their vertical
# (V-layer) track phase is identical by construction.  The vertical pitches are
# multiples of 504 = LCM(18, 18, 24, 56) — the unit pitches of M2/M4/M6/M8, the
# HORIZONTAL layers a cell may use under `reserve_top_layers 2` — so the
# horizontal phase is identical too.  Delta-y contributes ZERO track offset
# between stacked instances.
#
# `align_bottom_up` is NOT called here: the UNFLIPPED instances are already
# phase-aligned as placed (compare chip_bottomup.buda, which must nudge an SA
# placement onto a common phase first).  Drop --mirror-upper from the generator
# line and every cell reports ALIGNED.
#
# The 504 grid is only valid while the cells stay at or below M9: the full
# 10-layer stack would need LCM(18,18,24,56,74) = 18648, which for a 6300-tall
# cell is 12k of dead channel.  Reserving the top pair is what makes a tight
# on-grid pitch affordable — so this vehicle declares it.
#
# Regenerate the BDB with:
#   python3 tools/build_hier_demo.py flow/chip/chip_stack.bdb \
#     --cells big2=flow/big_data_test/big2/tc3b_flat_x5.buda,mix2=flow/rnr/mix2.bdb.sql \
#     --instances big2=2,mix2=4 --buses 100 \
#     --layout stacked --channel 1000 --grid 504 \
#     --column-align center --mirror-upper
#   python3 tools/bdb_serialize.py dump flow/chip/chip_stack.bdb flow/chip/chip_stack.bdb.sql
source chip_tracks_mirror.buda
open_bdb chip_stack.bdb.sql
set_bottom_up big2
set_bottom_up mix2
# No align_bottom_up: the placement is already on the track grid.
reserve_top_layers 2
derive_busterms 2
add_blocks_from_bdb 0
add_blocks_from_bdb 1 skip
add_blocks_from_bdb 2 skip
run_hier_bundler depth 2
generate_hier_topologies
run_planner hier 5 signal_tracks
run_nuts
check_template_tracks on_mismatch independent
run_detailed_nuts
report_wl
check_design
exit
